    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "Across the spectrum German outlets agree that the statutory pension is set to rise by roughly four percent in 2027, signalling a modest baseline adjustment rather than a major boost. They also uniformly stress that demographic ageing makes the current pay‑as‑you‑go system unsustainable, creating a consensus that some form of capital‑based supplement—whether Riester, private funds or a new capital pension—must be expanded while early retirement at 63 should be linked to reduced benefits to preserve intergenerational equity. Finally, despite divergent political framing, there is a shared narrative that any reform must balance fiscal responsibility with protecting retirees from poverty, limiting social contribution rates to avoid an excessive burden on the working generation.",
      "controversial_points": "The debate pits intergenerational equity against fiscal sustainability, with younger voters fearing higher contributions. Conservatives champion raising the retirement age to 70, arguing demographic pressure, while leftist parties demand stronger benefit guarantees. Market‑oriented media push for capital‑funded Riester alternatives, framing public pensions as outdated, whereas unions defend solidarity‑based schemes. Far‑right outlets exploit pension fears to attack immigration and EU policies, linking social spending to national decline."
